\nSELECT * FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id WHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\n## Scans the query forces\n\n1. **Full scan of `users`.** `lower(u.email)` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The database evaluates `lower()` on every row. This is the main problem.\n2. **Probable full scan of `orders`.** The join filters on `o.user_id`. If that column has no index, each matching user triggers a scan of `orders`, or the planner picks a hash join that reads all of `orders`. The schema isn't in the repo, so I can't confirm whether the index exists.\n3. **Wider reads than needed.** `SELECT *` pulls every column from both tables. That rules out index-only scans and sends extra data over the wire. Both tables also have columns with the same name (`id`, etc.), which can cause ambiguity in clients.\n\n## Fixes\n\n**Indexes (PostgreSQL syntax; adjust for your engine):**\n```sql\n-- expression index, so lower(email) can be looked up directly\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));\n\n-- supports the join from user to their orders\nCREATE INDEX orders_user_id_idx ON orders (user_id);\n```\nMake the first index UNIQUE only if emails must be unique case-insensitively. Otherwise use a plain index.\n\n**Alternative to the expression index:** store emails already normalized to lowercase, or use a `citext` column (Postgres) or a case-insensitive collation. Then you can query `u.email = 'a@example.org'` with an ordinary index on `email`.\n\n**Rewrite:**\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total   -- list the columns you need\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\nReplace the column list with what the caller actually uses. The join order doesn't change the plan in modern optimizers, but starting from the filtered table reads more clearly.\n\n## Expected result\nWith both indexes, the plan should be an index lookup on `users` that returns one row, followed by an index lookup on `orders` for that user. That replaces two table scans. Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after to confirm. I didn't run it because there's no database or schema in this workspace.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}